// result_end

\caption(Least squares over the 40-dimensional simplex. Left: error against step on log-log axes, with the theorem's bound \(4L/(k+2)\) in grey. Right: the first 200 steps on a log scale, where a straight line means linear convergence; errors below \(10^{-16}\) are drawn at \(10^{-16}\). Table: error after 10, 100 and 2,000 steps.)
\alttext(Left panel, log10 error from minus 17 to 6 against log10 k from 0 to 3.4. The grey bound falls from 2.5 to minus 0.4. Both vanilla curves stay below it: the 2 over k plus 2 curve, jagged, falls with slope about minus 2 to minus 4 at k equals 2000; the line-search curve, smooth, falls more slowly to minus 1.8. Away-step and pairwise curves drop steeply after log10 k of 1 and hit the floor near minus 14 to minus 16 by log10 k of 2. Right panel, the first 200 steps with linear k axis: pairwise falls as a nearly straight line to the floor at about k equals 70, away-step at about k equals 120, while both vanilla curves stay between 0 and minus 2.)

Every one of the 8,000 iterates obeys both Lemma 1 and the theorem's bound, which is loose by
three to four orders of magnitude here. Between \(k=100\) and \(2{,}000\) the line-search errors
fall with log-log slope about \(-0.8\), slower than \(1/k\), as Canon and Cullum's result
predicts. The standard step does better, slope about \(-1.8\), in line with the faster open-loop
rates of Wirth, Kerdreux and Pokutta. So line search made vanilla Frank-Wolfe *slower*: after
2,000 steps it is 180 times further from optimal. The theorem only promises that line search is
no worse in the bound, not in practice. The away-step and
pairwise errors fall on straight lines in the right panel, the linear convergence of
Lacoste-Julien and Jaggi, reaching \(10^{-12}\) after 101 and 63 steps.

> A dataset example: the nonparametric MLE of a mixture

`dm::datasets::faithful()` records 272 eruptions of the Old Faithful geyser; we model the waiting
time before each eruption, in minutes, which is bimodal. A *Gaussian location mixture* with known
\(\sigma\) has density \(\sum_j\pi_j\varphi_\sigma(x-\mu_j)\). The Kiefer-Wolfowitz *nonparametric
maximum likelihood estimate* (NPMLE) leaves the mixing distribution free: fix a fine grid of
locations \(\mu_1,\dots,\mu_J\), and choose the weights \(\pi\in\Delta_J\) to maximise the likelihood.
With \(\varphi_{ij}=\varphi_\sigma(x_i-\mu_j)\) and \(m_i(\pi)=\sum_j\pi_j\varphi_{ij}\) we minimise

$$
f(\pi)=-\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\log m_i(\pi),\qquad\frac{\partial f}{\partial\pi_j}=-D_j(\pi),\quad D_j(\pi)=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{\varphi_{ij}}{m_i(\pi)} .
$$
\alttext(f of pi equals minus 1 over n times the sum over i of log m i of pi; the partial derivative of f with respect to pi j equals minus D j of pi, where D j of pi equals 1 over n times the sum over i of phi i j over m i of pi.)

This is convex over the simplex, so Frank-Wolfe applies with `simplex_lmo`. Since
\(\sum_j\pi_jD_j(\pi)=\frac1n\sum_im_i/m_i=1\), the Frank-Wolfe gap is

$$
g(\pi)=\langle\nabla f(\pi),\pi-e_{j^\star}\rangle=\max_jD_j(\pi)-1 ,
$$
\alttext(g of pi equals the inner product of the gradient with pi minus e j star, which equals the maximum over j of D j of pi, minus 1.)

and Lemma 1 becomes Lindsay's (1983) optimality condition: \(\pi\) is the NPMLE exactly when
\(D_j(\pi)\le1\) for every \(j\). The classical EM algorithm for the weights is
\(\pi_j\leftarrow\pi_jD_j(\pi)\), which keeps the weights on the simplex by the same identity.
EM never sets a weight to zero, while the NPMLE is known to be discrete with few atoms (Lindsay
1983). Frank-Wolfe with line search is the "vertex direction method" of this literature, and the
pairwise variant is Böhning's (1986) "vertex exchange method".

`algorithms::gaussian_location_npmle` starts from equal weights on every grid point, each its
own atom, so that away and pairwise steps can drop grid points; `gaussian_location_em` runs EM
from the same start. We use \(\sigma=5\) minutes, a little below the spread within each of the two
clusters, a grid from 40 to 100 minutes in steps of one, a tolerance of \(10^{-6}\) on the gap and
at most 20,000 iterations.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/frank_wolfe_faithful.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto faithful = dm::datasets::faithful();
  const auto waiting = faithful.get_column("waiting").values();
  std::vector<double> grid;
  for (double t = 40; t <= 100; t += 1) grid.push_back(t);
  const double sigma = 5.0;

  struct Fit {
    std::string label;
    alg::MixingWeightsFit fit;
    dm::RGB colour;
  };
  std::vector<Fit> fits;
  const auto npmle = [&](alg::FrankWolfeVariant v, alg::FrankWolfeStep s) {
    return alg::gaussian_location_npmle(waiting, grid, sigma, {.max_iter = 20000, .tolerance = 1e-6, .variant = v, .step = s});
  };
  fits.push_back({"vanilla, 2/(k+2)", npmle(alg::FrankWolfeVariant::Vanilla, alg::FrankWolfeStep::Standard), {234, 88, 12}});
  fits.push_back({"vanilla, line search", npmle(alg::FrankWolfeVariant::Vanilla, alg::FrankWolfeStep::LineSearch), {217, 119, 6}});
  fits.push_back({"away-step", npmle(alg::FrankWolfeVariant::AwayStep, alg::FrankWolfeStep::LineSearch), {37, 99, 235}});
  fits.push_back({"pairwise", npmle(alg::FrankWolfeVariant::Pairwise, alg::FrankWolfeStep::LineSearch), {5, 150, 105}});
  fits.push_back({"EM", alg::gaussian_location_em(waiting, grid, sigma, 20000, 1e-6), {124, 58, 237}});

  std::vector<std::string> name, iters, loglik, gap, support;
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (const auto& [label, fit, colour] : fits) {
    name.push_back(label);
    iters.push_back(fit.converged ? std::to_string(fit.iterations) : std::format("> {}", fit.iterations));
    loglik.push_back(std::format("{:.6f}", fit.log_likelihood));
    gap.push_back(std::format("{:.1e}", fit.gap.back()));
    int positive = 0;
    for (const double w : fit.weights) positive += w > 1e-8;
    support.push_back(std::to_string(positive));
    std::vector<double> k, lg;
    for (double t = 0; t <= std::log10(double(fit.gap.size() - 1)); t += 0.02) {
      const auto i = static_cast<std::size_t>(std::round(std::pow(10.0, t)));
      k.push_back(std::log10(double(i)));
      lg.push_back(std::log10(std::max(fit.gap[i], 1e-12)));
    }
    right.lines(k, lg, label, colour, 2.0);
  }
  right.segments({0.0}, {-6.0}, {4.3}, {-6.0}, dm::RGB{100, 116, 139}, 1.0)
      .x_limits(0, 4.4)
      .y_limits(-7, 9)
      .title("Frank-Wolfe gap")
      .x_label("log10 iteration")
      .y_label("log10 (max D_j - 1)");

  const auto& best = fits[3].fit; // pairwise
  std::string atoms;
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.size(); ++j)
    if (best.weights[j] > 0) atoms += std::format("{}{:.0f} ({:.3f})", atoms.empty() ? "" : ", ", grid[j], best.weights[j]);

  // Density histogram, the fitted mixture density and the atoms (heights scaled to the weights).
  const double width = 2.5;
  std::vector<double> mids, dens;
  for (double lo = 40; lo < 100; lo += width) {
    int count = 0;
    for (const double w : waiting) count += w >= lo && w < lo + width;
    mids.push_back(lo + width / 2);
    dens.push_back(count / (waiting.size() * width));
  }
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (double x = 40; x <= 100; x += 0.25) {
    double v = 0;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.size(); ++j) v += best.weights[j] * std::exp(-0.5 * std::pow((x - grid[j]) / sigma, 2));
    xs.push_back(x);
    ys.push_back(v / (sigma * std::sqrt(2 * std::numbers::pi)));
  }
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.bars(mids, dens, "", dm::RGB{191, 219, 254}, width * 0.95).lines(xs, ys, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.size(); ++j)
    if (best.weights[j] > 0)
      left.segments({grid[j]}, {0.0}, {grid[j]}, {0.1 * best.weights[j]}, dm::RGB{5, 150, 105}, 3.0);
  left.x_limits(40, 100).y_limits(0, 0.06).title("NPMLE (pairwise), sigma = 5").x_label("waiting time (min)").y_label("density");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 500).fit_panels().save_svg("build/frank_wolfe_faithful.svg");

  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("method", name);
  table.add_column("iterations", iters);
  table.add_column("log-likelihood", loglik);
  table.add_column("final gap", gap);
  table.add_column("weights > 1e-8", support);
  std::print("Pairwise atoms, location (weight): {}.\n\n{}\n", atoms, table.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Pairwise atoms, location (weight): 52 (0.073), 53 (0.205), 62 (0.086), 63 (0.012), 79 (0.412), 80 (0.103), 86 (0.108).

| method | iterations | log-likelihood | final gap | weights > 1e-8 |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| vanilla, 2/(k+2) | > 20000 | -1032.391439 | 9.4e-05 | 24 |
| vanilla, line search | > 20000 | -1032.401997 | 1.1e-04 | 61 |
| away-step | 9480 | -1032.391418 | 9.8e-07 | 7 |
| pairwise | 3977 | -1032.391418 | 8.9e-07 | 7 |
| EM | 17827 | -1032.391429 | 1.0e-06 | 11 |

\image(figures/frank_wolfe_faithful.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Old Faithful waiting times. Left: density histogram, the NPMLE mixture density (black) and its atoms (green bars, height proportional to weight). Right: the Frank-Wolfe gap against iteration for each method, with the tolerance \(10^{-6}\) in grey. Table: iterations to the tolerance (at most 20,000), final log-likelihood, gap and number of weights above \(10^{-8}\).)
\alttext(Left panel: a histogram of waiting times from 43 to 96 minutes with two humps, near 53 and 80 minutes, the right one taller. A smooth black bimodal density follows the histogram, peaking at about 0.024 near 54 minutes and 0.045 near 80 minutes. Seven green bars mark the atoms at 52, 53, 62, 63, 79, 80 and 86 minutes; the tallest is at 79. Right panel: log10 gap from minus 7 to 9 against log10 iteration from 0 to 4.4. The vanilla standard-step curve starts near 9 and falls quickly to 0. All curves then decline roughly linearly; both vanilla curves end near minus 4 at 20,000 iterations, while pairwise drops steeply to minus 6 at about 4,000 iterations, away-step at about 9,500, and EM at about 18,000.)

All three converged methods agree on the log-likelihood to \(10^{-5}\), and the two that certify a
gap below \(10^{-6}\) with an exact active set agree to all six decimals: \(-1032.391418\). Neither
vanilla run reaches the tolerance in 20,000 steps. The line-search version is the vertex
direction method, whose slowness is the reason Böhning proposed the exchange method; it is also
stuck with every one of the 61 starting weights, since vanilla steps can only shrink them by
factors \((1-\gamma)\). The standard step jumps to a single grid point at its first step
(\(\gamma_0=1\)), so it starts sparse, but it still collects 24 points.

The pairwise solution has seven atoms in four groups: 52 and 53, 62 and 63, 79 and 80, and 86
minutes. Adjacent pairs are the grid's way of representing an atom that lies between two grid
points, so the mixing distribution has about four support points: the two clusters, each with a
shoulder. EM reaches the same likelihood within \(10^{-5}\) but leaves 11 weights above
\(10^{-8}\), because its multiplicative update shrinks unused weights geometrically and never sets
them to zero. EM's iterations are cheaper than pairwise ones, which need a line search, so EM's
4.5 times as many iterations do not make it slower in proportion. The difference is the answer: pairwise Frank-Wolfe returns the sparse
solution itself, with a certificate.

> Conclusion

Frank-Wolfe replaces projection with a linear minimisation, which for simplices, \(\ell_1\) balls
and vertex lists is a scan. The same oracle call gives a gap that bounds the error (Lemma 1),
and the standard step guarantees \(f(x_k)-f^\star\le2C_f/(k+2)\). Both held on every iterate we
checked. The rate is also the method's limit: when the optimum lies inside a face, the vanilla
method zig-zags and stays sublinear, and exact line search did not help, in two experiments it
made things worse. The away-step and pairwise variants fix this by keeping the active vertices and
moving weight off the bad ones. They reached machine precision in tens of steps on the triangle
and least-squares problems, and produced a sparse, certified NPMLE on the Old Faithful data.

Limits: the guarantees need a smooth objective. The mixture likelihood is not globally smooth
near the boundary of the simplex, which is why its vanilla runs are so slow. The away and pairwise
variants need the active set, which costs memory proportional to the number of vertices used. And
the LMO must be cheap: over sets where linear minimisation is as hard as projection, the method
loses its advantage.

> References

- Böhning, D. (1986). A vertex-exchange-method in D-optimal design theory. *Metrika*, 33, 337–347.
- Canon, M. D. and Cullum, C. D. (1968). A tight upper bound on the rate of convergence of Frank-Wolfe algorithm. *SIAM Journal on Control*, 6(4), 509–516.
- Frank, M. and Wolfe, P. (1956). An algorithm for quadratic programming. *Naval Research Logistics Quarterly*, 3(1–2), 95–110.
- Jaggi, M. (2013). Revisiting Frank-Wolfe: projection-free sparse convex optimization. *Proceedings of ICML*, 427–435.
- Kiefer, J. and Wolfowitz, J. (1956). Consistency of the maximum likelihood estimator in the presence of infinitely many incidental parameters. *Annals of Mathematical Statistics*, 27(4), 887–906.
- Lacoste-Julien, S. and Jaggi, M. (2015). On the global linear convergence of Frank-Wolfe optimization variants. *Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems* 28.
- Lindsay, B. G. (1983). The geometry of mixture likelihoods: a general theory. *Annals of Statistics*, 11(1), 86–94.
- Wirth, E., Kerdreux, T. and Pokutta, S. (2023). Acceleration of Frank-Wolfe algorithms with open-loop step-sizes. *Proceedings of AISTATS*, PMLR 206, 77–100.
- Wolfe, P. (1970). Convergence theory in nonlinear programming. In J. Abadie (ed.), *Integer and Nonlinear Programming*, 1–36. North-Holland.
